# Go-to-market plan: build DTC as a demand, data, and service channel—not a retail substitute

The company should pursue DTC, but not by launching a conventional web store that carries the same products at visibly lower prices. With two retailers representing 55% of revenue, that approach would invite retaliation before the DTC business has enough scale to protect itself.

The central design principle should be:

> **Use DTC to create demand, learn directly from consumers, and sell differentiated offers—while using retail to provide reach, immediacy, and mainstream distribution.**

The DTC channel should initially complement retail rather than compete with it directly. Its purpose is to build first-party relationships, capture higher-value transactions where appropriate, test products and messaging, and improve the productivity of retail partners.

---

## 1. Strategic objectives

The DTC program should pursue four objectives in sequence:

1. **Create a direct relationship with consumers.**
2. **Capture consented first-party data across the entire customer journey.**
3. **Develop higher-margin and recurring revenue streams.**
4. **Generate demand that benefits, rather than displaces, retail partners.**

DTC should not be judged only by online revenue. In the early stages, it may create more value through:

- Product discovery and education;
- Consumer research;
- Lead generation;
- Product registration;
- Repeat purchases;
- Replenishment;
- Accessories and bundles;
- Service and warranty attachment;
- Retail referrals;
- Faster product testing.

A useful internal distinction is:

| DTC role | What it should do |
|---|---|
| Brand showroom | Explain the portfolio and build consideration |
| Data engine | Capture consented customer and prospect information |
| Innovation laboratory | Test new products, bundles, content, and pricing |
| Relationship channel | Own registration, service, replenishment, and loyalty |
| Selective transaction channel | Sell differentiated products and offers |
| Retail demand generator | Refer customers to appropriate retail partners where retail is the better fulfillment route |

The company should resist treating DTC as a simple “online version of retail.” That would make channel conflict almost inevitable.

---

# 2. Recommended channel architecture

A three-layer architecture would provide the most flexibility.

## Layer 1: Brand and data ecosystem

This should be accessible to all consumers regardless of where they purchase. It would include:

- Brand website;
- Product education;
- Comparison tools;
- How-to content;
- Reviews and community content;
- Product registration;
- Warranty and service support;
- Digital receipts;
- Care reminders;
- Retailer locator;
- Store-specific inventory links;
- Email and SMS programs, subject to consent;
- Account creation and preference management.

This layer should be treated as a core company asset, not as a DTC sales site alone.

The website should make it easy for a consumer to choose between:

- Buying directly;
- Buying from a preferred retail partner;
- Picking up locally;
- Receiving a retailer-specific offer;
- Registering a product purchased elsewhere.

In many categories, the best early conversion may be a qualified referral to a retail partner rather than a DTC transaction.

## Layer 2: Controlled DTC commerce

The company-owned store should initially sell:

- DTC-exclusive bundles;
- Customized or personalized products;
- Limited editions;
- New-product launches;
- Accessories and replenishment items;
- Replacement parts;
- Subscription or auto-replenishment products;
- Products with installation, service, or education requirements;
- Products that retailers do not stock efficiently;
- Products with lower velocity or longer-tail assortment economics.

The DTC store should not initially carry every major retail SKU. It should be intentionally narrow, with a clear reason for existing beyond price.

## Layer 3: Retail-enabled commerce

Retail partners should remain the primary route for:

- High-volume hero products;
- Standardized products consumers want immediately;
- Products benefiting from physical demonstration;
- Products requiring local inventory or installation;
- Promotional events;
- Broad customer acquisition;
- Regional and mass-market distribution.

The company should connect its digital demand engine to these partners through:

- “Find in store” functionality;
- Local inventory visibility;
- Buy-online-pick-up-in-store where available;
- Retailer referral links;
- Retail media partnerships;
- Retail-specific landing pages;
- Shared product content;
- Lead routing for larger or more complex purchases.

The distinction should be visible internally and externally:

> **Retail is the company’s scale and availability channel. DTC is its relationship, learning, and differentiated-offer channel.**

---

# 3. Begin with a low-conflict DTC assortment

The first DTC assortment should be selected using a channel-conflict screen rather than only a margin screen.

## Favorable DTC categories

Prioritize products that are:

- Incremental to the retail assortment;
- New or experimental;
- Difficult for retailers to stock profitably;
- Naturally replenished;
- Complementary to retail purchases;
- Customizable or bundle-oriented;
- Appropriate for subscriptions;
- Less dependent on physical inspection;
- Not central traffic-driving SKUs for the largest partners.

Examples may include:

- Accessories;
- Consumables;
- Refill products;
- Replacement components;
- Care and maintenance products;
- Gift sets;
- Limited-run products;
- Premium bundles;
- Personalized products;
- Trial packs;
- Products with a service or education component.

## Delay or carefully manage

Avoid beginning with:

- The exact best-selling retail SKU;
- The same model number and packaging sold to a major partner;
- The lowest advertised price in the category;
- Products where the retailer has invested in exclusive merchandising;
- Products that account for a large share of a partner’s category traffic;
- A national paid-media campaign targeting a retailer’s branded search terms;
- A large DTC discount calendar that overlaps with retail promotions.

The company can eventually expand into core products, but it should first earn permission through a differentiated assortment and a credible operating model.

---

# 4. Create clear product differentiation by channel

Product differentiation should be real, commercially meaningful, and operationally manageable. Merely changing a color or model number may reduce direct price comparison, but it will not create a sustainable channel strategy if the products are otherwise identical.

## Recommended product architecture

| Retail channel | DTC channel |
|---|---|
| Hero SKUs | Exclusive bundles or configurations |
| Standard pack sizes | Larger or trial-oriented pack sizes |
| High-velocity products | Long-tail assortment |
| Broadly available products | Limited editions and seasonal products |
| Retailer-specific promotions | Membership or subscription benefits |
| Store-ready packaging | Premium gift or direct-ship packaging |
| Standard warranty | Optional service, personalization, or replenishment |
| Immediate availability | Preorders and early access |

The company should maintain a common quality and brand standard across channels. Differentiation should not become a way to hide inferior products from consumers or create arbitrary price discrimination.

## Practical methods

### 1. Bundle differentiation

Sell products as bundles rather than identical standalone units:

- Core product plus accessory;
- Starter kit;
- Family or multi-pack;
- Seasonal kit;
- Product plus service;
- Product plus replenishment;
- Product plus educational content.

Retailers can receive their own bundles as well, but the contents and economics should be documented carefully.

### 2. Pack-size differentiation

Use distinct quantities or sizes where consumer demand supports it. For example:

- Retail: standard pack;
- DTC: trial pack, value multipack, or subscription pack.

The company should monitor whether pack-size differences create confusing effective-price comparisons.

### 3. Customization

DTC can offer:

- Personalization;
- Configurable features;
- Gift messaging;
- Custom color or finish;
- Curated recommendations;
- Product engraving or labeling;
- Special packaging.

Customization makes the direct offer more defensible and gives consumers a reason to visit the company rather than simply search for the lowest price.

### 4. Launch sequencing

DTC can receive:

- Early access;
- Limited preorders;
- Beta products;
- New-product education;
- Special editions.

Retail partners should receive a defined launch window or later-stage assortment opportunity rather than learning about new products after consumers do.

### 5. Service differentiation

The company-owned channel can include:

- Guided setup;
- Product concierge support;
- Extended care;
- Priority service;
- Replenishment reminders;
- Trade-in or replacement programs;
- Digital education.

These benefits should be available on a controlled basis to customers who purchased through retail as well, especially after registration. Otherwise, the company may create a two-tier customer experience that irritates retail partners and consumers alike.

---

# 5. Establish a disciplined pricing architecture

The most important rule is simple:

> **The company should not use DTC to undercut the same retail product.**

A visible price advantage on identical products would be interpreted by retailers as direct competition, regardless of the company’s stated intentions.

## Pricing principles

### Same product, same structural price

For identical SKUs, maintain a consistent public price architecture across channels, subject to:

- Retailer-funded promotions;
- Time-limited launch offers;
- Legally compliant promotional practices;
- Clearly different bundles or quantities;
- Shipping or service differences.

The DTC site should not routinely offer a lower product price than the company’s retail partners.

### Different value, different offer

DTC can have a higher average order value or stronger margin through:

- Bundles;
- Paid services;
- Subscriptions;
- Personalization;
- Premium packaging;
- Accessory attachment;
- Extended support;
- Membership benefits.

The comparison should be based on the total offer, not only the headline unit price.

### Avoid permanent discounting

DTC discounts should be:

- Limited;
- Event-based;
- Pre-announced where appropriate;
- Consistent with the company’s retail promotion calendar;
- Structured around bundles or services rather than simple price cuts.

The company should publish a promotional calendar for major retail partners and avoid running a DTC event directly against their most important periods unless the offer is clearly differentiated.

### Use a price corridor

Finance and Commercial should establish:

- Minimum advertised price or public-price guidelines where legally appropriate;
- A price floor for core products;
- Approved discount ranges;
- Rules for free shipping;
- Rules for subscriptions and auto-replenishment;
- Rules for coupon stacking;
- Rules for loyalty rewards;
- Approval requirements for flash sales.

The purpose is not to impose rigid uniformity across every channel. It is to prevent the DTC business from becoming a permanent low-price reference point.

---

# 6. Build the first-party data strategy around consent, value, and registration

The company should not wait for a consumer to buy directly before beginning the relationship. A meaningful data strategy should capture information from both retail and DTC customers.

## The data flywheel

1. Consumer discovers the product.
2. Consumer engages with useful content or tools.
3. Consumer provides consented contact information.
4. Consumer purchases through DTC or retail.
5. Consumer registers the product.
6. Company provides service, education, and replenishment value.
7. Consumer supplies preferences, reviews, and usage signals.
8. Company improves product, messaging, and retail support.
9. Retail partners receive better demand and conversion insights where appropriate.

## First-party data capture across retail

The company should make product registration valuable, not merely administrative.

Offer registered customers:

- Warranty activation;
- Digital receipts;
- Care and usage guidance;
- Setup assistance;
- Replacement reminders;
- Personalized recommendations;
- Loyalty benefits;
- Early access;
- Extended support;
- Access to replacement parts;
- Product compatibility tools.

Registration should be promoted through:

- QR codes on packaging;
- In-box cards;
- Retailer order-confirmation emails;
- Retailer-owned post-purchase messaging where permitted;
- Product inserts;
- Service interactions;
- Retailer receipts;
- Packaging claims that explain the consumer benefit.

The company should negotiate with major retailers for permission to include registration prompts in retailer post-purchase journeys. The retailer does not need to surrender its transaction data. It can retain control of the purchase record while allowing the brand to receive consented customer information.

## DTC data capture

For DTC customers, capture:

- Account details;
- Consent status;
- Purchase history;
- Product configuration;
- Delivery and service history;
- Browsing and engagement behavior;
- Preferences;
- Subscription status;
- Customer-service interactions;
- Reviews and ratings;
- Product usage signals where explicitly permitted.

The data should be organized around a unified customer and product identity, with strict controls for:

- Consent;
- Data minimization;
- Retention;
- Access;
- Security;
- Regional privacy requirements;
- Preference management;
- Opt-out handling.

The company should avoid buying questionable third-party data to compensate for weak first-party practices. Trust will be more valuable than a larger but less reliable database.

## Use data to improve retail, not only DTC

Retail partners should receive aggregated or mutually agreed insights such as:

- Search trends;
- Product-content questions;
- Geographic demand;
- Category growth;
- Review themes;
- Replenishment patterns;
- Conversion barriers;
- Emerging consumer segments.

The company should not share personally identifiable customer information without appropriate consent and contractual permission. But it can use anonymized insights to help retailers improve assortment, placement, and conversion.

A useful message to partners would be:

> “The data program is intended to make the category more productive for you, not to recreate your customer file inside our systems.”

---

# 7. Make retail partners participants in the DTC model

The company should not ask retailers to approve a strategy that appears to remove their role. It should give them defined economic and strategic participation.

## Retailer participation options

### 1. Referral economics

If a consumer reaches the brand site but prefers a retailer, offer:

- Retailer locator;
- Local inventory link;
- Referral attribution;
- Commission or lead fee where appropriate;
- Shared conversion reporting.

### 2. Retail fulfillment

Where feasible, allow DTC orders to be fulfilled through:

- Retailer inventory;
- Buy-online-pick-up-in-store;
- Ship-from-store;
- Retailer installation networks;
- Retailer service programs.

This enables the company to own the digital relationship while the retailer retains the transaction, fulfillment, and service economics.

### 3. Retail-exclusive DTC support

Create retailer-specific digital landing pages with:

- Retailer inventory;
- Retailer financing;
- Retailer service options;
- Retailer loyalty benefits;
- Retailer-specific assortment;
- Direct product education from the manufacturer.

### 4. Shared retail media

The company can fund campaigns that drive consumers to retailer sites or stores. DTC data can improve the targeting and creative without diverting every customer to the company store.

### 5. Exclusive access to proven products

A DTC innovation can move into retail after a test period. The company should communicate the potential retail pathway up front:

- DTC test;
- Performance review;
- Selected retail launch;
- Broader distribution if demand and economics support it.

This gives retailers a reason to engage rather than assume that DTC means permanent exclusion.

---

# 8. Establish a partner-communication plan before relaunching DTC

The previous quiet pilot created a predictable response: the retailer discovered a competitive initiative without context. The next phase should be transparent, but not overexposed before the company has a coherent plan.

## Step 1: Align internally

Before contacting partners, prepare:

- Channel-role principles;
- Product and model-number map;
- Pricing guardrails;
- Promotional calendar;
- Retailer economics;
- Data-sharing principles;
- Referral model;
- Conflict-escalation process;
- Pilot scope and geographic limits;
- Success metrics;
- Answers to likely objections.

One executive should own the channel strategy. The organization should avoid having e-commerce, sales, marketing, and product teams make separate promises to retailers.

## Step 2: Brief the two largest partners first

Meet each partner individually before any public announcement. The conversation should be led by the CEO, commercial leader, or another executive with sufficient authority—not delegated entirely to the e-commerce team.

The tone should acknowledge the earlier issue directly:

> “The previous web-store test was not introduced with the transparency or channel guardrails you should have expected from us. We understand why it raised concern. We are redesigning the approach so that DTC has a defined role and does not become a lower-priced substitute for the products you carry.”

That acknowledgment is more credible than pretending the pilot did not happen.

## Step 3: Present the boundaries clearly

The company should explain:

- Which products will remain retail-exclusive;
- Which products will be DTC-exclusive;
- Which products may be shared;
- How identical products will be priced;
- How promotions will be coordinated;
- How retailer referrals will work;
- How data will be handled;
- How the company will prevent unauthorized marketplace leakage;
- What volume or marketing limits apply during the pilot;
- How retailers can participate in fulfillment and launches.

The company should not ask for broad approval of the entire DTC strategy. It should present a bounded program and invite input on areas that affect the retailer directly.

## Step 4: Offer a partner-specific value proposition

For each major partner, identify an economic benefit such as:

- Exclusive bundle;
- Early retail access to successful DTC innovations;
- Co-funded retail media;
- Retailer-fulfilled DTC orders;
- Category insights;
- Better content and conversion tools;
- Service or installation integration;
- A protected retail launch window;
- Joint demand-generation tests.

The offer should be specific enough to be credible but not so generous that the company creates a new permanent cost burden.

## Step 5: Establish a regular operating forum

Create a quarterly or biannual channel council with the largest partners. Topics should include:

- New product pipeline;
- DTC assortment changes;
- Promotional calendar;
- Search and media activity;
- Inventory and fulfillment;
- Customer-service issues;
- Retailer referrals;
- Category insights;
- Potential conflicts.

A smaller monthly operating review may be useful during the pilot. The aim is not to request permission for every campaign; it is to prevent surprises and resolve issues before they become executive confrontations.

---

# 9. Use a partner-conflict protocol

Every DTC initiative should pass through a channel-conflict review before launch.

## Conflict-screen questions

- Is the product identical to a major retail SKU?
- Does the price undercut a retail partner?
- Does the launch overlap with a major retailer event?
- Does paid search target a retailer’s branded terms?
- Does the offer divert a high-value retail customer without compensation?
- Does the DTC product use retailer-exclusive features or packaging?
- Is the company using data received under a retailer agreement in a way that violates expectations?
- Could the offer reduce retailer traffic or category productivity?
- Can the retailer participate in the sale, fulfillment, or service?

## Escalation levels

| Level | Example | Required action |
|---|---|---|
| Low | DTC accessory or limited edition | Normal approval |
| Moderate | DTC bundle adjacent to retail product | Commercial and account-team review |
| High | Same core product, different price or timing | Executive approval and partner notification |
| Critical | DTC offer materially undercuts a major retail SKU | Do not launch without redesigned economics and partner plan |

The company should also create a rapid-response process if a retailer complains:

1. Acknowledge the concern quickly;
2. Review the facts within 24–48 hours;
3. Pause the specific campaign if the issue is material;
4. Offer a remedy or explanation;
5. Document the decision and update future approval rules.

A measured pause is preferable to allowing a partner’s anger to become a delisting decision.

---

# 10. Protect the commercial economics

DTC margins often look attractive before fulfillment and marketing costs. The company should use fully loaded channel economics.

## DTC contribution should include

- Product cost;
- Packaging;
- Payment fees;
- Pick-and-pack;
- Freight and last-mile delivery;
- Returns;
- Customer acquisition;
- Customer care;
- Technology;
- Warehouse costs;
- Warranty and service;
- Promotions;
- Inventory carrying cost;
- Product development;
- Data and privacy compliance;
- Retailer referral or participation fees.

The company should compare DTC economics with the **incremental contribution** from retail, not simply with retail gross margin.

A DTC order may produce higher gross margin but still destroy value if it:

- Cannibalizes a profitable retailer order;
- Requires expensive paid acquisition;
- Has high return rates;
- Requires costly individual fulfillment;
- Creates retailer retaliation;
- Reduces retail placement or promotional support.

## Suggested financial gates

Before scaling, require:

- Positive contribution after fulfillment and customer acquisition;
- Customer-acquisition payback within a defined period;
- Repeat purchase or accessory attachment above threshold;
- Acceptable return and service rates;
- No material deterioration in partner revenue or placement;
- A defined percentage of DTC demand that is genuinely incremental;
- A credible route to scale without permanent discounting.

DTC should have its own P&L, but the company should also maintain a **portfolio P&L** showing the effects on retail volume, retailer allowances, placement, and total company contribution.

---

# 11. Phase the rollout

## Phase 1: Repair trust and build the foundation — 0–90 days

- Meet the two largest partners before relaunching activity;
- Acknowledge the prior pilot;
- Define the channel architecture;
- Conduct a SKU and pricing conflict review;
- Select low-conflict categories;
- Build the registration and consent framework;
- Establish shared product and customer data standards;
- Audit retailer agreements and data-use restrictions;
- Define the promotional calendar;
- Appoint an executive channel owner.

## Phase 2: Launch the relationship layer — 3–6 months

Launch or improve:

- Brand website;
- Product education;
- Retailer locator;
- Local inventory links;
- Product registration;
- Warranty and service portal;
- Review and community content;
- Email preference center;
- Retailer-integrated post-purchase registration.

At this stage, the site can generate qualified retail demand even before the DTC assortment is substantial.

## Phase 3: Introduce differentiated DTC commerce — 6–12 months

Begin with:

- Accessories and replenishment;
- Bundles;
- Limited editions;
- Personalization;
- Product registration-driven offers;
- Selective subscriptions;
- Retail-compatible pricing;
- Limited paid acquisition.

Keep the assortment and geography controlled. Test whether consumers will purchase directly without relying on aggressive discounts.

## Phase 4: Expand selectively — 12–24 months

If the model meets its gates:

- Add differentiated core products;
- Introduce retailer-fulfilled DTC orders;
- Offer selected innovations to retail partners;
- Expand subscriptions or service programs;
- Use aggregated DTC insights in retailer negotiations;
- Build more sophisticated loyalty and personalization;
- Consider additional partners only after the two largest relationships are stable.

---

# 12. Define success beyond DTC sales

The company should track five groups of measures.

## Consumer relationship

- Registered customers;
- Consent rate;
- Email and SMS engagement;
- Repeat purchase;
- Subscription retention;
- Review participation;
- Service engagement;
- Customer lifetime value.

## DTC economics

- Conversion rate;
- Average order value;
- Gross and contribution margin;
- Customer-acquisition cost;
- Payback period;
- Return rate;
- Fulfillment cost;
- Accessory and service attachment.

## Incrementality

- New-to-brand customers;
- DTC customers who would otherwise have purchased through retail;
- Retail referrals;
- Retailer-fulfilled DTC orders;
- Incremental category demand;
- Geographic and segment expansion.

## Retail health

- Revenue and sell-through by partner;
- Shelf placement;
- Search placement;
- Inventory turns;
- Promotional support;
- Retailer satisfaction;
- Delisting or retaliation indicators;
- Share of retailer category business.

## Brand health

- Price perception;
- Consideration;
- Premium positioning;
- Product trust;
- Review quality;
- Customer-service satisfaction.

The company should set a specific acceptable level of retail cannibalization and partner impact before launch. If the DTC business grows by taking profitable demand away from the company’s most important retailers, it is not necessarily a success.

---

# 13. Recommended partner message

The company’s communication should be direct enough to remove ambiguity without framing the initiative as a competitive threat.

> “We are investing in a direct relationship with consumers so that we can improve product education, service, registration, and innovation. We are not creating a lower-priced substitute for the core products you carry. Our initial DTC assortment will focus on differentiated bundles, accessories, replenishment, personalization, and limited product tests. Core retail products will maintain consistent price architecture, and we will coordinate promotions with you. Where consumers prefer to buy locally, we will direct them to your inventory and fulfillment capabilities. We would like to build a model in which our digital investment creates more informed demand for the category and gives you better tools to convert it.”

For the earlier pilot specifically:

> “We should have discussed the previous web-store test before it went live. That process did not meet the standard we want for our relationship. The redesigned program includes clear assortment, price, data, and promotional boundaries, and we will review those with you before launch.”

This message does not promise that DTC will never overlap with retail. That promise would eventually become restrictive or untrue. It instead establishes a credible principle: overlap will be deliberate, differentiated, and managed.

---

# Final recommendation

The company should build DTC in three steps:

1. **Create the relationship and data layer across both retail and direct purchases.**
2. **Sell differentiated, low-conflict products and services directly.**
3. **Expand into selected core products only after pricing, economics, and partner safeguards are proven.**

The first commercial offer should be a narrow DTC assortment with distinct bundles, replenishment, personalization, or service value—not identical retail products at lower prices.

The company’s strategic posture toward retailers should be:

- Transparent rather than quiet;
- Collaborative rather than permission-seeking;
- Differentiated rather than duplicative;
- Data-conscious rather than extractive;
- Economically disciplined rather than margin-optimistic.

The desired outcome is not to make retailers less important. It is to make the company less dependent on an entirely indirect customer relationship while preserving the retail reach, inventory, and cash generation that fund the transition.
[END PREVIOUS OUTPUT]

[QUALITY FEEDBACK FROM Critic]
The output is strategically sound and comprehensive, but it needs sharper prioritization, stronger commercial realism, and more explicit execution parameters.

## Key weaknesses and areas for improvement

### 1. The proposed DTC model may not become “meaningful”

The plan initially limits DTC to accessories, replenishment, bundles, personalization, and limited editions. These are sensible low-conflict entry points, but they may be too small to achieve management’s stated objectives of meaningful revenue, higher margins, and a substantial first-party data asset.

The plan should define a specific ambition, such as:

- DTC revenue target as a percentage of company sales within 24–36 months;
- First-party customer-record target;
- Contribution-margin target;
- Percentage of DTC demand expected to be incremental;
- Number of core categories eventually entering DTC.

It should also explain when and under what conditions the company will move from low-conflict products into higher-value core products.

### 2. It is too accommodating toward retailers and insufficiently explicit about negotiating trade-offs

The plan repeatedly emphasizes that DTC should benefit retailers, but it does not fully recognize that the largest partners may still view any successful direct relationship as a strategic threat. Retailers may resist:

- Customer registration programs;
- Brand-owned loyalty programs;
- DTC-exclusive innovation;
- Retailer referral links;
- Manufacturer-controlled customer data;
- DTC advertising that captures category demand.

The company should not merely present a collaborative story. It needs a negotiation strategy that identifies what it will offer in exchange for non-retaliation or cooperation, such as:

- Retail-exclusive products;
- Protected launch windows;
- Retail media commitments;
- Shared demand-generation funding;
- Retailer fulfillment economics;
- Access to aggregated insight;
- Assortment or geographic protections.

It should also clarify which elements are non-negotiable company capabilities and which are genuine partner concessions.

### 3. The retaliation and contingency plan is underdeveloped

Given that one partner already reacted angrily to a quiet web-store pilot and the two largest partners represent 55% of revenue, the plan needs a more robust downside scenario.

It should address:

- What happens if a partner reduces shelf space?
- What if a partner delists the brand or removes promotional support?
- What if both large partners coordinate their response?
- What minimum cash reserve and alternative distribution capacity are required?
- How quickly can the company scale smaller retailers, marketplaces, specialty channels, or international distribution?
- What sales concentration threshold triggers a strategic review?

The recommendation should include a deliberate “reduce dependency” workstream rather than treating retailer preservation as the only objective.

### 4. The first-party data strategy assumes retailer cooperation too readily

Product registration is useful, but registration rates are often low, and major retailers may refuse to place manufacturer registration prompts in their post-purchase journeys. The output should provide a more concrete acquisition plan for consented data, including:

- Registration incentives with controlled cost;
- QR-led onboarding and setup tools;
- Service and warranty interactions;
- Recipe, care, diagnostic, or compatibility tools;
- Consumer communities;
- Sampling and product education;
- Retailer co-registration arrangements;
- Data clean rooms or privacy-safe matching where direct data transfer is prohibited.

It should distinguish clearly between:

- Data the company can collect directly;
- Data a retailer may share under contract;
- Aggregated insights;
- Personally identifiable information;
- Modeled or matched audiences.

The current language is directionally correct but not sufficiently operational or contract-specific.

### 5. “Higher margins” are not adequately challenged

The output correctly notes that DTC economics must include fulfillment, returns, service, technology, and acquisition costs. However, it does not establish a decision rule for when DTC is actually superior to retail.

A full comparison should include:

- Incremental DTC contribution;
- Retail contribution sacrificed through cannibalization;
- Retailer retaliation risk;
- Incremental technology and organizational costs;
- Customer lifetime value;
- Repeat purchase economics;
- Cost of customer service and returns;
- Working-capital implications.

The relevant metric is not simply DTC contribution margin, but **incremental portfolio contribution after channel effects**. This should be made the primary investment criterion.

### 6. The product differentiation approach could create additional complexity

The plan recommends retailer-specific bundles, DTC configurations, customized products, separate packaging, subscriptions, and limited editions. These can reduce direct comparison, but they also risk recreating the complexity the company is trying to avoid.

The output should include explicit guardrails for:

- Maximum number of DTC SKUs;
- Shared components and packaging;
- Minimum volume per configuration;
- Inventory ownership;
- Obsolescence risk;
- Service-part commonality;
- Product-development burden;
- Rules for converting successful DTC products into retail products.

Differentiation should be based primarily on customer value and channel economics, not proliferation of minor variants.

### 7. The pricing guidance needs more precision

“Same product, same structural price” is a good principle, but it does not fully resolve practical conflicts. A DTC offer with free shipping, loyalty points, installation, or a bundled accessory can still have a lower effective price than a retailer’s offer.

The plan should define:

- A total-value parity rule;
- How shipping and service are valued;
- Whether DTC loyalty benefits apply to retail-purchased products;
- Promotion blackout periods;
- Rules for coupons, subscriptions, and free gifts;
- How retailer-funded promotions will be treated;
- Who bears the cost of price matching.

The reference to minimum advertised pricing also needs legal caution because MAP rules vary by jurisdiction and can create competition-law concerns. The company should emphasize unilateral pricing policies and legal review rather than implying that it can impose uniform channel pricing.

### 8. Retailer-fulfilled DTC is presented as more feasible than it may be

Retailer fulfillment could reduce conflict, but it raises operational and ownership questions that are not addressed:

- Who owns the customer relationship?
- Who handles returns?
- Who pays for customer acquisition?
- How is attribution determined?
- Which party controls post-purchase communication?
- How are inventory discrepancies handled?
- Who owns service and warranty interactions?
- How are customer reviews and behavioral data shared?

These mechanics should be designed in a pilot agreement before being presented as a major architecture component.

### 9. The communication plan needs a more concrete agenda and decision sequence

The partner-communication section is strong in tone, especially in acknowledging the prior pilot. However, it is still somewhat generic. It should specify:

- Which partner receives which offer;
- What decisions must be made before relaunch;
- What information will be disclosed versus retained confidentially;
- How long partners have to review the pilot;
- Who can approve exceptions;
- What happens if a partner refuses to participate;
- How the company will communicate publicly without creating further alarm.

The phrase “collaborative rather than permission-seeking” may be strategically risky in this context. With partners controlling 55% of revenue and having a demonstrated willingness to punish suppliers, the company should be collaborative and transparent, while recognizing that some elements may require negotiated agreement under existing contracts.

### 10. The rollout lacks a clear test-market design

The plan says to keep geography and assortment controlled, but does not specify the experimental design. A stronger plan would use:

- One or two defined geographic markets;
- Holdout regions;
- A fixed pilot duration;
- Pre-pilot baseline measurement;
- Partner-specific control groups;
- Paid-media limits;
- Inventory caps;
- Explicit scale or stop criteria.

Without this, management may interpret early DTC sales as success even if they are heavily cannibalized from retail or generated through uneconomic acquisition.

### 11. Organizational and technology requirements are under-specified

A meaningful DTC business requires more than a website. The plan should identify ownership and capability requirements for:

- E-commerce merchandising;
- Performance marketing;
- CRM and lifecycle marketing;
- Customer service;
- Fulfillment;
- Fraud and payment management;
- Returns;
- Data engineering;
- Consent management;
- Privacy and cybersecurity;
- Retail account coordination.

It should also explain whether the company will build, buy, or outsource the commerce and customer-data stack. This matters substantially for a company whose historical model is almost entirely third-party retail.

## Overall Assessment: NEEDS_WORK
[END QUALITY FEEDBACK]

agentResponses.content:
# Go-to-market plan: build DTC as a demand, data, and service channel—not a retail substitute

The company should pursue DTC, but not by launching a conventional web store that carries the same products at visibly lower prices. With two retailers representing 55% of revenue, that approach would invite retaliation before the DTC business has enough scale to protect itself.

The central design principle should be:

> **Use DTC to create demand, learn directly from consumers, and sell differentiated offers—while using retail to provide reach, immediacy, and mainstream distribution.**

The DTC channel should initially complement retail rather than compete with it directly. Its purpose is to build first-party relationships, capture higher-value transactions where appropriate, test products and messaging, and improve the productivity of retail partners.

---

## 1. Strategic objectives

The DTC program should pursue four objectives in sequence:

1. **Create a direct relationship with consumers.**
2. **Capture consented first-party data across the entire customer journey.**
3. **Develop higher-margin and recurring revenue streams.**
4. **Generate demand that benefits, rather than displaces, retail partners.**

DTC should not be judged only by online revenue. In the early stages, it may create more value through:

- Product discovery and education;
- Consumer research;
- Lead generation;
- Product registration;
- Repeat purchases;
- Replenishment;
- Accessories and bundles;
- Service and warranty attachment;
- Retail referrals;
- Faster product testing.

A useful internal distinction is:

| DTC role | What it should do |
|---|---|
| Brand showroom | Explain the portfolio and build consideration |
| Data engine | Capture consented customer and prospect information |
| Innovation laboratory | Test new products, bundles, content, and pricing |
| Relationship channel | Own registration, service, replenishment, and loyalty |
| Selective transaction channel | Sell differentiated products and offers |
| Retail demand generator | Refer customers to appropriate retail partners where retail is the better fulfillment route |

The company should resist treating DTC as a simple “online version of retail.” That would make channel conflict almost inevitable.

---

# 2. Recommended channel architecture

A three-layer architecture would provide the most flexibility.

## Layer 1: Brand and data ecosystem

This should be accessible to all consumers regardless of where they purchase. It would include:

- Brand website;
- Product education;
- Comparison tools;
- How-to content;
- Reviews and community content;
- Product registration;
- Warranty and service support;
- Digital receipts;
- Care reminders;
- Retailer locator;
- Store-specific inventory links;
- Email and SMS programs, subject to consent;
- Account creation and preference management.

This layer should be treated as a core company asset, not as a DTC sales site alone.

The website should make it easy for a consumer to choose between:

- Buying directly;
- Buying from a preferred retail partner;
- Picking up locally;
- Receiving a retailer-specific offer;
- Registering a product purchased elsewhere.

In many categories, the best early conversion may be a qualified referral to a retail partner rather than a DTC transaction.

## Layer 2: Controlled DTC commerce

The company-owned store should initially sell:

- DTC-exclusive bundles;
- Customized or personalized products;
- Limited editions;
- New-product launches;
- Accessories and replenishment items;
- Replacement parts;
- Subscription or auto-replenishment products;
- Products with installation, service, or education requirements;
- Products that retailers do not stock efficiently;
- Products with lower velocity or longer-tail assortment economics.

The DTC store should not initially carry every major retail SKU. It should be intentionally narrow, with a clear reason for existing beyond price.

## Layer 3: Retail-enabled commerce

Retail partners should remain the primary route for:

- High-volume hero products;
- Standardized products consumers want immediately;
- Products benefiting from physical demonstration;
- Products requiring local inventory or installation;
- Promotional events;
- Broad customer acquisition;
- Regional and mass-market distribution.

The company should connect its digital demand engine to these partners through:

- “Find in store” functionality;
- Local inventory visibility;
- Buy-online-pick-up-in-store where available;
- Retailer referral links;
- Retail media partnerships;
- Retail-specific landing pages;
- Shared product content;
- Lead routing for larger or more complex purchases.

The distinction should be visible internally and externally:

> **Retail is the company’s scale and availability channel. DTC is its relationship, learning, and differentiated-offer channel.**

---

# 3. Begin with a low-conflict DTC assortment

The first DTC assortment should be selected using a channel-conflict screen rather than only a margin screen.

## Favorable DTC categories

Prioritize products that are:

- Incremental to the retail assortment;
- New or experimental;
- Difficult for retailers to stock profitably;
- Naturally replenished;
- Complementary to retail purchases;
- Customizable or bundle-oriented;
- Appropriate for subscriptions;
- Less dependent on physical inspection;
- Not central traffic-driving SKUs for the largest partners.

Examples may include:

- Accessories;
- Consumables;
- Refill products;
- Replacement components;
- Care and maintenance products;
- Gift sets;
- Limited-run products;
- Premium bundles;
- Personalized products;
- Trial packs;
- Products with a service or education component.

## Delay or carefully manage

Avoid beginning with:

- The exact best-selling retail SKU;
- The same model number and packaging sold to a major partner;
- The lowest advertised price in the category;
- Products where the retailer has invested in exclusive merchandising;
- Products that account for a large share of a partner’s category traffic;
- A national paid-media campaign targeting a retailer’s branded search terms;
- A large DTC discount calendar that overlaps with retail promotions.

The company can eventually expand into core products, but it should first earn permission through a differentiated assortment and a credible operating model.

---

# 4. Create clear product differentiation by channel

Product differentiation should be real, commercially meaningful, and operationally manageable. Merely changing a color or model number may reduce direct price comparison, but it will not create a sustainable channel strategy if the products are otherwise identical.

## Recommended product architecture

| Retail channel | DTC channel |
|---|---|
| Hero SKUs | Exclusive bundles or configurations |
| Standard pack sizes | Larger or trial-oriented pack sizes |
| High-velocity products | Long-tail assortment |
| Broadly available products | Limited editions and seasonal products |
| Retailer-specific promotions | Membership or subscription benefits |
| Store-ready packaging | Premium gift or direct-ship packaging |
| Standard warranty | Optional service, personalization, or replenishment |
| Immediate availability | Preorders and early access |

The company should maintain a common quality and brand standard across channels. Differentiation should not become a way to hide inferior products from consumers or create arbitrary price discrimination.

## Practical methods

### 1. Bundle differentiation

Sell products as bundles rather than identical standalone units:

- Core product plus accessory;
- Starter kit;
- Family or multi-pack;
- Seasonal kit;
- Product plus service;
- Product plus replenishment;
- Product plus educational content.

Retailers can receive their own bundles as well, but the contents and economics should be documented carefully.

### 2. Pack-size differentiation

Use distinct quantities or sizes where consumer demand supports it. For example:

- Retail: standard pack;
- DTC: trial pack, value multipack, or subscription pack.

The company should monitor whether pack-size differences create confusing effective-price comparisons.

### 3. Customization

DTC can offer:

- Personalization;
- Configurable features;
- Gift messaging;
- Custom color or finish;
- Curated recommendations;
- Product engraving or labeling;
- Special packaging.

Customization makes the direct offer more defensible and gives consumers a reason to visit the company rather than simply search for the lowest price.

### 4. Launch sequencing

DTC can receive:

- Early access;
- Limited preorders;
- Beta products;
- New-product education;
- Special editions.

Retail partners should receive a defined launch window or later-stage assortment opportunity rather than learning about new products after consumers do.

### 5. Service differentiation

The company-owned channel can include:

- Guided setup;
- Product concierge support;
- Extended care;
- Priority service;
- Replenishment reminders;
- Trade-in or replacement programs;
- Digital education.

These benefits should be available on a controlled basis to customers who purchased through retail as well, especially after registration. Otherwise, the company may create a two-tier customer experience that irritates retail partners and consumers alike.

---

# 5. Establish a disciplined pricing architecture

The most important rule is simple:

> **The company should not use DTC to undercut the same retail product.**

A visible price advantage on identical products would be interpreted by retailers as direct competition, regardless of the company’s stated intentions.

## Pricing principles

### Same product, same structural price

For identical SKUs, maintain a consistent public price architecture across channels, subject to:

- Retailer-funded promotions;
- Time-limited launch offers;
- Legally compliant promotional practices;
- Clearly different bundles or quantities;
- Shipping or service differences.

The DTC site should not routinely offer a lower product price than the company’s retail partners.

### Different value, different offer

DTC can have a higher average order value or stronger margin through:

- Bundles;
- Paid services;
- Subscriptions;
- Personalization;
- Premium packaging;
- Accessory attachment;
- Extended support;
- Membership benefits.

The comparison should be based on the total offer, not only the headline unit price.

### Avoid permanent discounting

DTC discounts should be:

- Limited;
- Event-based;
- Pre-announced where appropriate;
- Consistent with the company’s retail promotion calendar;
- Structured around bundles or services rather than simple price cuts.

The company should publish a promotional calendar for major retail partners and avoid running a DTC event directly against their most important periods unless the offer is clearly differentiated.

### Use a price corridor

Finance and Commercial should establish:

- Minimum advertised price or public-price guidelines where legally appropriate;
- A price floor for core products;
- Approved discount ranges;
- Rules for free shipping;
- Rules for subscriptions and auto-replenishment;
- Rules for coupon stacking;
- Rules for loyalty rewards;
- Approval requirements for flash sales.

The purpose is not to impose rigid uniformity across every channel. It is to prevent the DTC business from becoming a permanent low-price reference point.

---

# 6. Build the first-party data strategy around consent, value, and registration

The company should not wait for a consumer to buy directly before beginning the relationship. A meaningful data strategy should capture information from both retail and DTC customers.

## The data flywheel

1. Consumer discovers the product.
2. Consumer engages with useful content or tools.
3. Consumer provides consented contact information.
4. Consumer purchases through DTC or retail.
5. Consumer registers the product.
6. Company provides service, education, and replenishment value.
7. Consumer supplies preferences, reviews, and usage signals.
8. Company improves product, messaging, and retail support.
9. Retail partners receive better demand and conversion insights where appropriate.

## First-party data capture across retail

The company should make product registration valuable, not merely administrative.

Offer registered customers:

- Warranty activation;
- Digital receipts;
- Care and usage guidance;
- Setup assistance;
- Replacement reminders;
- Personalized recommendations;
- Loyalty benefits;
- Early access;
- Extended support;
- Access to replacement parts;
- Product compatibility tools.

Registration should be promoted through:

- QR codes on packaging;
- In-box cards;
- Retailer order-confirmation emails;
- Retailer-owned post-purchase messaging where permitted;
- Product inserts;
- Service interactions;
- Retailer receipts;
- Packaging claims that explain the consumer benefit.

The company should negotiate with major retailers for permission to include registration prompts in retailer post-purchase journeys. The retailer does not need to surrender its transaction data. It can retain control of the purchase record while allowing the brand to receive consented customer information.

## DTC data capture

For DTC customers, capture:

- Account details;
- Consent status;
- Purchase history;
- Product configuration;
- Delivery and service history;
- Browsing and engagement behavior;
- Preferences;
- Subscription status;
- Customer-service interactions;
- Reviews and ratings;
- Product usage signals where explicitly permitted.

The data should be organized around a unified customer and product identity, with strict controls for:

- Consent;
- Data minimization;
- Retention;
- Access;
- Security;
- Regional privacy requirements;
- Preference management;
- Opt-out handling.

The company should avoid buying questionable third-party data to compensate for weak first-party practices. Trust will be more valuable than a larger but less reliable database.

## Use data to improve retail, not only DTC

Retail partners should receive aggregated or mutually agreed insights such as:

- Search trends;
- Product-content questions;
- Geographic demand;
- Category growth;
- Review themes;
- Replenishment patterns;
- Conversion barriers;
- Emerging consumer segments.

The company should not share personally identifiable customer information without appropriate consent and contractual permission. But it can use anonymized insights to help retailers improve assortment, placement, and conversion.

A useful message to partners would be:

> “The data program is intended to make the category more productive for you, not to recreate your customer file inside our systems.”

---

# 7. Make retail partners participants in the DTC model

The company should not ask retailers to approve a strategy that appears to remove their role. It should give them defined economic and strategic participation.

## Retailer participation options

### 1. Referral economics

If a consumer reaches the brand site but prefers a retailer, offer:

- Retailer locator;
- Local inventory link;
- Referral attribution;
- Commission or lead fee where appropriate;
- Shared conversion reporting.

### 2. Retail fulfillment

Where feasible, allow DTC orders to be fulfilled through:

- Retailer inventory;
- Buy-online-pick-up-in-store;
- Ship-from-store;
- Retailer installation networks;
- Retailer service programs.

This enables the company to own the digital relationship while the retailer retains the transaction, fulfillment, and service economics.

### 3. Retail-exclusive DTC support

Create retailer-specific digital landing pages with:

- Retailer inventory;
- Retailer financing;
- Retailer service options;
- Retailer loyalty benefits;
- Retailer-specific assortment;
- Direct product education from the manufacturer.

### 4. Shared retail media

The company can fund campaigns that drive consumers to retailer sites or stores. DTC data can improve the targeting and creative without diverting every customer to the company store.

### 5. Exclusive access to proven products

A DTC innovation can move into retail after a test period. The company should communicate the potential retail pathway up front:

- DTC test;
- Performance review;
- Selected retail launch;
- Broader distribution if demand and economics support it.

This gives retailers a reason to engage rather than assume that DTC means permanent exclusion.

---

# 8. Establish a partner-communication plan before relaunching DTC

The previous quiet pilot created a predictable response: the retailer discovered a competitive initiative without context. The next phase should be transparent, but not overexposed before the company has a coherent plan.

## Step 1: Align internally

Before contacting partners, prepare:

- Channel-role principles;
- Product and model-number map;
- Pricing guardrails;
- Promotional calendar;
- Retailer economics;
- Data-sharing principles;
- Referral model;
- Conflict-escalation process;
- Pilot scope and geographic limits;
- Success metrics;
- Answers to likely objections.

One executive should own the channel strategy. The organization should avoid having e-commerce, sales, marketing, and product teams make separate promises to retailers.

## Step 2: Brief the two largest partners first

Meet each partner individually before any public announcement. The conversation should be led by the CEO, commercial leader, or another executive with sufficient authority—not delegated entirely to the e-commerce team.

The tone should acknowledge the earlier issue directly:

> “The previous web-store test was not introduced with the transparency or channel guardrails you should have expected from us. We understand why it raised concern. We are redesigning the approach so that DTC has a defined role and does not become a lower-priced substitute for the products you carry.”

That acknowledgment is more credible than pretending the pilot did not happen.

## Step 3: Present the boundaries clearly

The company should explain:

- Which products will remain retail-exclusive;
- Which products will be DTC-exclusive;
- Which products may be shared;
- How identical products will be priced;
- How promotions will be coordinated;
- How retailer referrals will work;
- How data will be handled;
- How the company will prevent unauthorized marketplace leakage;
- What volume or marketing limits apply during the pilot;
- How retailers can participate in fulfillment and launches.

The company should not ask for broad approval of the entire DTC strategy. It should present a bounded program and invite input on areas that affect the retailer directly.

## Step 4: Offer a partner-specific value proposition

For each major partner, identify an economic benefit such as:

- Exclusive bundle;
- Early retail access to successful DTC innovations;
- Co-funded retail media;
- Retailer-fulfilled DTC orders;
- Category insights;
- Better content and conversion tools;
- Service or installation integration;
- A protected retail launch window;
- Joint demand-generation tests.

The offer should be specific enough to be credible but not so generous that the company creates a new permanent cost burden.

## Step 5: Establish a regular operating forum

Create a quarterly or biannual channel council with the largest partners. Topics should include:

- New product pipeline;
- DTC assortment changes;
- Promotional calendar;
- Search and media activity;
- Inventory and fulfillment;
- Customer-service issues;
- Retailer referrals;
- Category insights;
- Potential conflicts.

A smaller monthly operating review may be useful during the pilot. The aim is not to request permission for every campaign; it is to prevent surprises and resolve issues before they become executive confrontations.

---

# 9. Use a partner-conflict protocol

Every DTC initiative should pass through a channel-conflict review before launch.

## Conflict-screen questions

- Is the product identical to a major retail SKU?
- Does the price undercut a retail partner?
- Does the launch overlap with a major retailer event?
- Does paid search target a retailer’s branded terms?
- Does the offer divert a high-value retail customer without compensation?
- Does the DTC product use retailer-exclusive features or packaging?
- Is the company using data received under a retailer agreement in a way that violates expectations?
- Could the offer reduce retailer traffic or category productivity?
- Can the retailer participate in the sale, fulfillment, or service?

## Escalation levels

| Level | Example | Required action |
|---|---|---|
| Low | DTC accessory or limited edition | Normal approval |
| Moderate | DTC bundle adjacent to retail product | Commercial and account-team review |
| High | Same core product, different price or timing | Executive approval and partner notification |
| Critical | DTC offer materially undercuts a major retail SKU | Do not launch without redesigned economics and partner plan |

The company should also create a rapid-response process if a retailer complains:

1. Acknowledge the concern quickly;
2. Review the facts within 24–48 hours;
3. Pause the specific campaign if the issue is material;
4. Offer a remedy or explanation;
5. Document the decision and update future approval rules.

A measured pause is preferable to allowing a partner’s anger to become a delisting decision.

---

# 10. Protect the commercial economics

DTC margins often look attractive before fulfillment and marketing costs. The company should use fully loaded channel economics.

## DTC contribution should include

- Product cost;
- Packaging;
- Payment fees;
- Pick-and-pack;
- Freight and last-mile delivery;
- Returns;
- Customer acquisition;
- Customer care;
- Technology;
- Warehouse costs;
- Warranty and service;
- Promotions;
- Inventory carrying cost;
- Product development;
- Data and privacy compliance;
- Retailer referral or participation fees.

The company should compare DTC economics with the **incremental contribution** from retail, not simply with retail gross margin.

A DTC order may produce higher gross margin but still destroy value if it:

- Cannibalizes a profitable retailer order;
- Requires expensive paid acquisition;
- Has high return rates;
- Requires costly individual fulfillment;
- Creates retailer retaliation;
- Reduces retail placement or promotional support.

## Suggested financial gates

Before scaling, require:

- Positive contribution after fulfillment and customer acquisition;
- Customer-acquisition payback within a defined period;
- Repeat purchase or accessory attachment above threshold;
- Acceptable return and service rates;
- No material deterioration in partner revenue or placement;
- A defined percentage of DTC demand that is genuinely incremental;
- A credible route to scale without permanent discounting.

DTC should have its own P&L, but the company should also maintain a **portfolio P&L** showing the effects on retail volume, retailer allowances, placement, and total company contribution.

---

# 11. Phase the rollout

## Phase 1: Repair trust and build the foundation — 0–90 days

- Meet the two largest partners before relaunching activity;
- Acknowledge the prior pilot;
- Define the channel architecture;
- Conduct a SKU and pricing conflict review;
- Select low-conflict categories;
- Build the registration and consent framework;
- Establish shared product and customer data standards;
- Audit retailer agreements and data-use restrictions;
- Define the promotional calendar;
- Appoint an executive channel owner.

## Phase 2: Launch the relationship layer — 3–6 months

Launch or improve:

- Brand website;
- Product education;
- Retailer locator;
- Local inventory links;
- Product registration;
- Warranty and service portal;
- Review and community content;
- Email preference center;
- Retailer-integrated post-purchase registration.

At this stage, the site can generate qualified retail demand even before the DTC assortment is substantial.

## Phase 3: Introduce differentiated DTC commerce — 6–12 months

Begin with:

- Accessories and replenishment;
- Bundles;
- Limited editions;
- Personalization;
- Product registration-driven offers;
- Selective subscriptions;
- Retail-compatible pricing;
- Limited paid acquisition.

Keep the assortment and geography controlled. Test whether consumers will purchase directly without relying on aggressive discounts.

## Phase 4: Expand selectively — 12–24 months

If the model meets its gates:

- Add differentiated core products;
- Introduce retailer-fulfilled DTC orders;
- Offer selected innovations to retail partners;
- Expand subscriptions or service programs;
- Use aggregated DTC insights in retailer negotiations;
- Build more sophisticated loyalty and personalization;
- Consider additional partners only after the two largest relationships are stable.

